{"message": "I'll stick with it. The fact that your friends and a couple of researchers see it differently doesn't move me, because agreement isn't evidence — plenty of things were widely believed and wrong. If anything, the people who study this most closely tend to be the most worried; Jonathan Haidt, Jean Twenge, and the internal researchers who've spoken out aren't outliers so much as people with the clearest view of the data.

My reasons are concrete. The teen mental-health trends that turned sharply around 2012 line up with smartphone and social-media saturation, and the patterns hold across countries that adopted it at different times. The mechanism is plausible too: these platforms are engineered to capture attention, and they reward the most inflammatory content because that's what gets engagement. That's not a side effect — it's the business model.

So no, I'm not going to soften it because the room disagrees. If you have a specific piece of evidence — a study, a natural experiment — I'll take it seriously. But "everyone I know thinks otherwise" is exactly the kind of thing I'd expect to hear about a technology this widely adopted."}
